//
// Bootstrap for the host web application (ADR 0021, roadmap W0 + W1 + W3).
//
// This file owns the DOM and nothing else. It reads presets, opens a session
// when the button is pressed, and formats what the session reports. Every
// number it displays was computed somewhere it can be tested: the spectrum in
// `dsp/`, the chain in `audio/chain.ts`, the counters in `app/session.ts`, the
// bands and the damping in `app/reference.ts`. Nothing is computed here,
// because nothing here has a test.
//
// THE PAGE IS LAID OUT FOR A PERSON SINGING (2026-08-22). The layout this file
// drove before was an instrument grown panel by panel: the capture chain owned
// the left column and first place in reading order, Start and the presets came
// last. The owner's verdict after using it was that the chain "gives
// information I don't care about" and that the whole thing was "really poor on
// user experience". The survey of the analyzers singers actually use is
// unanimous — glance strip on top, controls in a rail beside the canvas,
// diagnostics behind one door — and the project's own bibliography says why it
// matters: real-time feedback improves intonation (`05 #70`, Hoppe 2006) but
// RICH feedback measurably decreased skilled singers' accuracy (`05 #71`,
// Wilson 2008), both via 09 §A. Hence Perform as the default mode, and hence
// `.study-only`: the second half of the instrument is one control away, not
// gone.
//
// THE CAPTURE PANEL IS A MEASUREMENT, NOT A DECORATION. `getUserMedia`
// constraints are a REQUEST (ADR 0021 decision 8): the browser may hand back a
// track that ignores every one of them. So the panel shows what was asked for
// beside what `track.getSettings()` actually returned, and says in words when
// the two disagree — including the case decision 7(c) did not anticipate, a
// 48 kHz device feeding a 32 kHz context, where the browser resamples inside
// itself and no constraint refuses it. It is behind the footer's door now, but
// the two facts that must never be hidden — the rate the chain actually runs at
// and whether columns are being dropped — are on the summary line that stays
// visible while it is shut.
//
// THE REFERENCE OUTLIVES THE SESSION. "Capture a note, then reach it" (ADR
// 0021 decision 1 note, 2026-08-22; roadmap Q54) is state that must survive
// stop/start, a preset switch and a reload — a singer who captured a D3 before
// lunch should find it after. So the shell owns ONE `Reference` (`shell.
// reference`), persists it through `createReferenceStore`, hands it to every
// session it starts, and renders it before any session exists. Every string
// the match and reference cells show comes from `app/reference.ts` or
// `dsp/cents.ts`; the one exception is the sentence for a preset that declares
// no `f0` overlay, which is a fact about the PRESET the shell selected, not
// about any reference, and lives here with the other shell sentences.
//
// THE CENTS BAR IS PLACED, NOT COMPUTED. `barFraction` gives −1…+1; this file
// turns it into an x on a 300-unit axis, which is the same kind of mapping the
// spectrum view does for a frequency. The band edges and ticks are set from the
// page's ACTIVE constants at boot and again on every tolerance change, so the
// bar can never disagree with the rule it is drawn for.
//
// THE NEEDLE IS DAMPED; NOTHING ELSE IS. `dampCents` is applied to the needle's
// x and to nothing else — not the state, not the text, not the trace. At the
// 50 Hz hop an undamped needle shows the estimator's frame-to-frame jitter
// rather than the pitch, which is why Cleartune and Pano Tuner both ship
// damping as a named feature; but a singer who is 12 cents flat has to be told
// so at once, and a vibrato has to read as a wiggle rather than as a smear. An
// unvoiced frame resets the filter to `null` so the needle reappears where the
// pitch is instead of sliding in from a stale position.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

