//
// The live session: the one place that owns a running capture.
//
// Everything below it is a pure or single-purpose module — `openCapture` opens
// a microphone, `spectrum.worker.ts` transforms a hop, `spectrum-view.ts`
// paints a column. None of them knows about the others. This file is the wire
// between them, and it is deliberately the ONLY file that holds the three at
// once, so that "what is running right now" has a single answer.
//
// THE ORDER OF TEARDOWN IS LOAD-BEARING. Stop the worklet's port first, then
// the worker, then the tracks, then the context. Reversed, a hop posted between
// `context.close()` and the port handler being detached lands on a worker whose
// `postMessage` target is gone, and the browser reports it as an unhandled
// rejection with no stack that points here.
//
// COUNTERS ARE MEASURED, NOT DERIVED. `expectedColumnsPerSecond` is what the
// preset ASKS for (`fs/hop`); `achievedColumnsPerSecond` is frames actually
// posted divided by elapsed wall time. ADR 0021 decision 3 forbids quoting
// either as a property of the watch, and the whole point of keeping both is
// that the gap between them is visible rather than assumed away.
//
// THE REFERENCE LIVES HERE TOO, BUT IS NOT BORN HERE. "Capture a note, then
// reach it" (ADR 0021 decision 1 note, 2026-08-22; roadmap Q54) needs the last
// `holdFrames` frames kept somewhere, and the session is the only object that
// sees every frame. So it keeps a ring of them and hands the ring to
// `app/reference.ts` — the pure module that decides whether the hold is
// capturable, what its median f0 is, and how far the live f0 is from it. This
// file never computes a cents figure: `onMatch` relays what `matchState`
// returned, per frame, and `onReadiness` what `captureReadiness` returned,
// every fifth frame (the button's enabled state does not need 50 Hz).
//
// The ring holds the `FrameMessage` objects themselves. Every array in one of
// them is a FRESH allocation the Worker transferred and never touches again
// (`protocol.ts`, `frameTransferables`), so holding twenty-five of them aliases
// nothing that can change under the capture — which is the property a block
// mean of raw periodograms (ADR 0006 D8) needs from its inputs.
//
// The ghost is drawn only when the reference is COMPARABLE with the running
// preset (`compatibility`): a spectrum averaged at N = 4096 on one window and
// scaling has no place on the axes of N = 8192 on another. The pitch is still
// compared — a cents figure is a ratio of two frequencies and does not care
// which preset measured either — so the match cell keeps working and the
// reference cell carries the warning instead.
//
// FIVE PANES, ONE HEAD (2026-08-22). The spectrum, the spectrogram, the overlay
// on top of it, the axis strip between them and the pitch strip are all fed
// from this one message handler, and three of them index history by ROW. The
// row is the spectrogram's — `waterfall.push` returns the ring slot it wrote —
// and it is handed to the overlay and the pitch strip in the same statement, so
// a dropped or refused frame cannot drift the f0 trace off the harmonics it
// belongs to. Nothing here derives a row of its own; that is the whole reason
// `trace-ring.ts` has no counter.
//
// THE TOLERANCE IS THE SINGER'S, AND IT LIVES HERE (2026-08-22). `matchState`
// has always taken its constants; what is new is that the page can change them
// mid-session. `setTolerance` rebuilds the frozen constants through
// `withTolerance`, re-derives the LANE the overlay and the pitch strip draw,
// recomputes the last frame's match under the new band and reports it — so the
// glance cell, the meter, the two lanes and the session's own `lastMatch` can
// never be reading different bands. `barSpanCents` is deliberately not part of
// it: the needle's scale must not move under a singer who only changed what
// counts as in tune.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

